#1 \par
\par
     Alysha twisted the sword, catching the glint of the overhead lights in its plastic edges. Her brows lifted. "Are you sure this is wise, Taylitha?"\par
\par
     Across the room, the woman sitting on the bench laughed as she tied back her scarlet hair. "Wise?" she said. "I doubt it. Necessary, though... that I believe, sir." She stood and paced across the room, her slender shape shorter than Alysha's even at the tops of her pointed brown ears. There was only determination in her dark blue eyes, though, when she came to face her captain on the mat, plastic sword in hand. "Are you ready?"\par
\par
     "I suppose," Alysha said, shaking back her hair and lifting her blade. "Though I'm telling you, I'm not going to need to win yet another sword fight crucial to the security and well-being of the Alliance any time soon."\par
\par
     "But when you do, I'm not going to have to sit through a live broadcast of you getting cut up," Taylitha said. "Now, watch your guard!"\par
\par
     Alysha let out a breath in something approaching a sigh and let Taylitha come for her. They exchanged blows--one, two, three--and Alysha's practice sword flew to the other side of the mat.\par
\par
     Taylitha scowled at Alysha's hand blocking the edge of her blade. "I know it worked on Gledig Colony, sir, but that's the last time anyone's going to let you get away with that trick. Particularly since the footage of your duel with Dylan's been broadcast to every known corner of the universe."\par
\par
     "Was it every known corner?" Alysha asked, amused. At Taylitha's renewed frown, she laughed and said, "You're right, of course. It's just habit."\par
\par
     "If this had been a bastard sword, or An-forbid, a claymore, you wouldn't have any more fingers."\par
\par
     "If that had been a claymore, arii, you wouldn't have been wielding it like a long sword."\par
\par
     Taylitha leaned back and folded her arms, sword sticking out of the crook of one elbow. "Are you going to take this seriously or not? I have a vested interest, professional and personal, in seeing you in one piece, Alysha Forrest. It was a nightmare to me and every one of your senior personnel to have to watch Dylan Brushnie, sword-dancer worthy of Le Ceau Tournament, go after you in Daleth's crumbled courtyard while you tried to uphold the honor of the Alliance... and have him nearly take you to pieces."\par
\par
     "It was only a few scratches," Alysha said.\par
\par
     "A few scratches? Almost bisecting your quadriceps? What about your arm? Not to mention your hands after you caught his final downswing. I thought Laelkii was going to have a coronary."\par
\par
     Alysha smiled, chagrined. "I survived, didn't I?"\par
\par
     "Barely," Taylitha said, tail lashing. She sighed and chuckled. "And with far less style than is your wont. Look, like it or not, sword-fighting is still widespread in the Core and the colonies. It's accepted as the traditional dueling weapon. It's still used and taught at Fleet Academe. Swords are legal in places where palmers and other projectile weapons aren't. Would you rather walk into those places armed or naked?"\par
\par
     Alysha rubbed her wrist. "I'm never without weapons, Taylitha."\par
\par
     Taylitha paused, glanced at the taller woman's fingertips. "Maybe. But a little distance from your enemy is a useful thing."\par
\par
     "Is it?"\par
\par
     An exasperated sigh escaped Taylitha. "Everything's philosophy with you, sir. Put up your cursed sword and we'll talk about it while I beat some muscle memory into your arms."\par
\par
     "Done," Alysha said, and jumped forward, sword swinging.\par
\par
     Taylitha swayed backward, blade meeting hers with a dull thump. "So tell me again about your intimacy problems."\par
\par
     "What?" Alysha said after a surprised laugh. She ducked her first commander's next thrust.\par
\par
     "Don't waste so much motion. Time is blood." Taylitha stepped a few paces away, then tapped her blade a few times. "Your intimacy problems with weapons. Swords are just as intimate a weapon as hands... maybe more so."\par
\par
     "More so?" Alysha arched a brow.\par
\par
     "Watch my body, not the sword." Taylitha tossed her matted bangs off her forehead. "Because there's more elegance. It stresses the times you come close by contrast."\par
\par
     "We're normally distant, Taylitha. Getting a little personal isn't a bad thing," Alysha said, transferring her attention from the woman's sword-tip to her arm. "I like to look into the eyes of a person I'm hurting. It makes me... more responsible."\par
\par
     "More responsible, or more likely to frenzy?"\par
\par
     Alysha hesitated, and grunted as Taylitha slapped her in the side with her plastic blade. "I suppose," she said, sidestepping the next swing, "That depends on the kind of person you are. I'm finding it hard to look into your eyes when I'm busy tracking your arm."\par
\par
     "Why is it any different for sword fighting, sir? Watch my eyes."\par
\par
     Alysha drew in a breath and pressed the offensive. Taylitha met her blade easily. "Because there's a stick on the end of your arm." When the smaller woman laughed, Alysha grinned and continued, "In all seriousness, a sword--any weapon--is a barrier. If you're going to be responsible for someone's pain, you should feel it yourself."\par
\par
     "Don't you think that'll make you neurotic?" Taylitha tapped her on the shoulder. Her parries had the grace of a tournament champion, which Alysha wryly admired. "Face it, throughout history people have been objectifying the enemy. Otherwise, it becomes too difficult to kill him. And then there are mental problems--who can hold 'that which I must destroy' and 'him who has family and his own concerns' in her head at the same time and not go insane?"\par
\par
     Alysha paused, and Taylitha waved the point of the sword at her. "Don't stop. You spent your entire bout with Dylan talking philosophy. If this is going to be a habit of yours, we might as well reproduce the conditions while practicing."\par
\par
     "Taylitha, I am not a swordswoman."\par
\par
     "Not yet. But I'm getting you there. You've stopped dodging so dramatically."\par
\par
     "Dramatically!" Alysha chuckled. "I am not dramatic!"\par
\par
     "You move like a dancer when you're not checking yourself," Taylitha said, cocking her head. "It's a little too much grace and a little too little econo--ah!"\par
\par
     Alysha smacked her on the hip and thrust for her first commander's head. Taylitha's wild swing struck the plastic blade, which broke in two pieces with a piercing crack.\par
\par
     They stared together at the hilt left in Alysha's hand.\par
\par
     Taylitha said, "That was not in the plan."\par
\par
     A faint smile curved Alysha's lips. "Swords do shatter."\par
\par
     "I didn't think my parry was that hard!"\par
\par
     They both glanced at Alysha's wrist, dented by the thin cords of ligaments that ran to muscle up her arm. Taylitha sighed and took the captain by the upper arm and led her off the mat. "You have to think less in terms of resistance and more in terms of flexibility. Respond only as forcefully as you need to in order to succeed. Anything more is wasted energy, wasted motion."\par
\par
     "Wasted swords," Alysha added blithely. "What now?"\par
\par
     "Now...," Taylitha tossed her plastic blade aside and grabbed a towel, "A beer."\par
\par
     Alysha shook her head. "Am I so difficult that I drive you to drinking?"\par
\par
     "Oh, no, no. I've always been a lush. You just exacerbate my problem."\par
\par
     "Taylitha," Alysha said, letting a sober note touch her alto. The smaller woman glanced at her, one brown ear canted toward her. "I promise I'll take it seriously. Let's meet every other day here, before dawn shift."\par
\par
     The first commander's mouth quirked into a smile. "Done. Come to the Fang with me?"\par
\par
     "Sure."\par
\par
     They left the gym for the corridors of the UAV Stardancer, a 400-person feminic battlecruiser in the Fleet of the United Alliance crewed only by females (and the occasionally neuter). Her complement had been weighted toward the felinesque races of the Alliance, Harat-Shar, Asanii and Karaka'An, but she had a smaller mix of others: the fox-based Tam-illee, and wolf-based Hinichi; the mix-and-matched Aera; the centauroid Glaseah... and of course, humans, now ubiquitous throughout Alliance space. Walking among the trees that helped comprise the Stardancer's naturalized oxygen-exchange system, Alysha and Taylitha passed double handfuls of women who nodded to them politely or waved.\par
\par
     Their destination was open: the Fang bistro was always open. Restaurant, bar and cafe, it served all the Stardancer's shifts. The ship's designers had placed it, out of expedience or cruelty, within sight of the gym. Popular vote leaned toward 'cruelty'.\par
\par
     "You'll be wanting your normal table, First Commander?" the slim Tam-illee foxine asked, ears pricking as they rounded the corner. "Oh, Captain! Good evening."\par
\par
     "Good evening, Sashe. So she has a regular table, ah?"\par
\par
     The foxine's lashes fluttered and she grinned. "Same as you do, when you come, sir."\par
\par
     Taylitha covered her face in mock dismay. "The table, Sashe. Before she starts grilling me on my caloric intake."\par
\par
     Sashe glanced at Alysha's enviable waist, then said, "Right away, Commander."\par
\par
     Alysha glanced at Taylitha after they'd been seated in a corner with a window. "Your caloric intake?"\par
\par
     Taylitha contrived an innocent expression. "A body wants tiramisu every once in a while. And beer."\par
\par
     "Not together, I hope."\par
\par
     Taylitha laughed. "No, not together."\par
\par
     They gave their orders to an ensign and sat together in companionable silence, listening to the murmur of conversation. The Fang never lacked for patrons; besides events scheduled by the jazz band or the Happy Houris, there wasn't much to do off-duty except exercise, eat and (for the unlucky) catch up on paperwork.\par
\par
     Their waitress slid a beer stein across the table along with Alysha's cup of coffee. "Anything else? We have a special today... apple dumplings. Real apples, too, from the new grafts on deck twelve."\par
\par
     "Oh, sure," Taylitha said. "Bring a couple. Thanks, Ensign."\par
\par
     Alysha shook her head, sipped her coffee.\par
\par
     "Don't tell me you don't like apples."\par
\par
     "They're good. I'll eat one, if that's what you're asking."\par
\par
     Taylitha grinned. "I guess I am." She propped her bare feet up on one of the remaining chairs and leaned back. "I do wonder how you learned to dance when you move, sir."\par
\par
     Alysha hesitated, put down her coffee. "I didn't think I was that--"\par
\par
     "Dramatic?" Taylitha supplied.\par
\par
     "Yes," Alysha said, then shook her head. "I suppose you learn to make the most of your body language when you're around those who understand bodies better than words."\par
\par
     One of Taylitha's red brows rose. "Like...?"\par
\par
     "Like Flitzbe. Naysha. Platyhelminthes."\par
\par
     "Mmm," Taylitha said. "I haven't had much contact with any of the three, though I've talked to a few Naysha through an interpreter."\par
\par
     Alysha nodded. "I bunked with a clod of Flitzbe as a lieutenant commander on the UAV ----------."\par
\par
     "You... bunked with them?" Taylitha stared at her, not even glancing at the steaming dumplings deposited on the table.\par
\par
     Alysha leaned over and speared one of them with a knife. She dropped it on the small plate provided with the dessert and separated a chunk of apple and dough with a fork. "They had limited cabin space for officers in my grade when I came aboard. I could take a lieutenant's bunk, or room with the Flitzbe, which they'd put in a commander's cabin with open walls to the water environment. I preferred the larger space, so I took it."\par
\par
     "What was it like?" Taylitha asked, pulling the other dumpling over. "An, the things don't even eat."\par
\par
     "It was actually quite pleasant," Alysha said. "They're interesting creatures. Some of them are as small as your hand... others are as large as a beach ball. The neural 'fur' is very soft, and has a slight grip. And they 'talk' by hooking together into piles and passing messages along the fur. They liked to clamber together on me... I think they were always a little disappointed that I couldn't share my thoughts the way they did among themselves." She chuckled. "But they didn't leave food around. They didn't shed. They didn't produce waste, or noise. And their idea of camaraderie with the large, silent biped was to curl up against me while I was sleeping or reading."\par
\par
     Taylitha squinted. "Did you ever communicate with them, meaningfully?"\par
\par
     "You could get sensations from them, if you pressed yourself to their fur . . . but they were rarely coherent, and never verbal. Images, maybe, recycled from your own memories and strung together to try and evoke some response."\par
\par
     Taylitha shook her head. "I never had much call to work with them. People without a language concept . . . how do you handle that?"\par
\par
     Alysha grinned. "Language isn't the only communication, arii. It might in fact be the youngest of the ways we convey meaning to one another."\par
\par
     "Even so. It's the way I understand best."\par
\par
     They watched the people passing in companionable silence, the remains of the dumplings on their plates. Taylitha finished off her beer and stretched. "I'm on-duty in ten minutes, so I guess I'll get moving."\par
\par
     "Mmm. Make sure Alastar gets some rest if she looks tired," Alysha said. "I think she forgets she has a body when we get on these mapping expeditions."\par
\par
     Taylitha chuckled. "I'll try. I won't make any promises, though, particularly if there's anything interesting in this new system we're on the cusp of."\par
\par
     Alysha laughed. "Fair enough! I'll see you in a few hours."\par
\par
     "Be well, sir."\par
\par
     The first commander wove her way through the tables to the Fang's exit, brown tail waving behind her. Alysha smiled and sipped her coffee, content to listen to the talk of her crew. There was an area of quiet immediately around her table, and people walking through it nodded to her before quickly moving on. She found it difficult to gauge their reactions to her. The Stardancer had had other captains before her, and during the first six months the crew attitude had seemed cautious, deferent. After the incident on Gledig, the deference had become more marked. Alysha wasn't certain if she liked it or not, but she supposed it was not the job of the captain to be liked. Respect would have to do.\par
\par
     Finishing her coffee, Alysha stood and strolled out of the Fang, heading toward her quarters beneath the fronds of the corridor's willowblend trees. Their current fact-finding assignment gave everyone time for relaxation, with the science databases receiving new data with each light year they covered. The engineering section had its own share of the fun: in charge of creating and deploying the Well booster relays the ship dropped behind it to facilitate real-time communications with the Alliance several quadrants away. There'd been a contest earlier to come up with the best design and decoration to preserve the utilitarian function of the buoy housing while still marking the Stardancer as its deploying ship; Alysha had dropped in to congratulate the contestants and examine the winning entry a few weeks back. The engineers had even managed to improve the repeater design in the bargain.\par
\par
     Alysha stepped into her rooms, padding quietly to the bed in the back and dropping onto it. In the window beside the bed a star cast a brilliant yellowed light, its center masked by a dark gray film provided by the smart coating on the inside of the flexglass.\par
\par
     "What star is that?"\par
\par
     A name tagged the star in the window, written in blue sans serif letters: 'Theth-129.'\par
\par
     "New data for this system?"\par
\par
     Two more tags appeared, floating near the star. Alysha squinted, then chuckled. "A planet and a moon? Oh, Alastar won't be sleeping for days." The tags didn't display any supporting information: what kind of planet and moon, only orbital information in the orange that indicated uncertainty. They were probably too distant still to gather any of that information. It would be the sixth planet they'd cataloged on this journey so far.\par
\par
     The tags slowly faded from the plexglass surface, leaving only the gray halo masking the star. Alysha folded her arms behind her back and stared out the window.\par
\par
     She had been fortunate that Taylitha had accepted her explanation about the Flitzbe as the reason for her highly developed 'dramatics'. It wasn't an implausible reason, for all it wasn't the real one: the Flitzbe did not parse language at all, so telling them her intentions to move to the kitchen, to wake up, to use the bathing chamber had been in vain. Telegraphing her motions helped them roll out of her way.\par
\par
     That cruise had been a pleasant one. Her studies in xenolinguistics had prepared her intellectually for communicating with other species, but nothing really taught like a protracted experience dealing with the realities of it. She wished she could contact the Flitzbe she'd become acquainted with; she was reasonably sure she could identify them as individuals, having sit with them enough to get a flavor of their sendings. But no one had yet constructed an interface for the Flitzbe to use communication systems . . . perhaps because no one knew if they wanted them.\par
\par
     Alysha rolled onto her side, back to the window, brushing her dark hair off her neck. The Flitzbe were far more pleasant to consider than dancing, anyway.\par
\par
