\par
 \par
\par
     "Captain to the bridge!"\par
\par
     Alysha started awake to find the gray disk had darkened, become almost opaque and twice as large as it had been. "What is it, Alastar?"\par
\par
     "We've found an anomaly on the planet in orbit around Theth-129."\par
\par
     Alysha slid out of bed and ran a quick comb through her hair, straightened her uniform. "Chronolog," she murmured to her dresser. The time popped up on the mirror--she'd been asleep almost two hours. "What kind of anomaly?"\par
\par
     "We think . . . they're ruins."\par
\par
     "On my way."\par
\par
     The captain's quarters had been positioned one deck below and almost beneath the bridge. Less than a minute later Alysha exited the lift onto the second level of the Stardancer's bridge. In the two-story holographic display a green and yellow planet rotated in serene silence, whorls of gray clouds uncurling slowly across its surface.\par
\par
     "What do we have?" she asked.\par
\par
     "It looks like ruins," Alastar said from the science station. The gray-brown Karaka'An turned from the display and pointed to a smaller projection over her board. It enlarged with dizzying speed until the image hung over what looked like an obelisk and several stones arranged around it, edges heavily sharpened by the computer's resolution-enhancing routines.\par
\par
     "Margin of error is twenty percent on these images," Alastar said. "But even so, this is obviously a structure."\par
\par
     "Agreed," Alysha said. "And twenty percent is reason enough to investigate further. What's the atmosphere like down there?"\par
\par
     "Good for breathing. We're not picking up any pathogens, but I've had the Medplex and Engineering rig the standard remote sensor array to double-check."\par
\par
     "Have them send it down," Alysha said, leaning over Alastar's shoulder and staring at the ruins.\par
\par
     "Pad Room 1, this is the Bridge. Send down the array."\par
\par
     "Aye, sir."\par
\par
     A new window opened on Alastar's screen. She dragged it to the solidigraphic emitter and widened the resulting floating box as readings began to scroll through it in glowing blue. One by one, blue tags popped up beside it with floating discs indicating different areas.\par
\par
     When all the tags connected and flashed, Alastar tapped them with a finger tip. "Medical and Science readings indicate everything clears. It'll be a little hot and dry for comfort, but not dangerously so."\par
\par
     Alysha frowned at the medical read-outs, scanning them herself. "Have you assembled a team?"\par
\par
     "Not yet."\par
\par
     "I'd like you to go. Take some science people. At least one engineer. Also a healer and a few security people. We've had no signs of life at all?"\par
\par
     "What looks like animals. We haven't been able to find any people that might be responsible for erecting something like this, nor any atmospheric signs of a manufacturing society. No orbital debris indicative of a space-faring civilization either. The indications are not favorable."\par
\par
     "We might have come too late to meet these people," Alysha murmured.\par
\par
     Alastar nodded, then stood. "I'll be in communication with you."\par
\par
     "Make sure you follow both standard procedures, for possible first contact and possible archeological sites." Alysha smiled. "Doesn't hurt to be prepared, just in case."\par
\par
     "Just in case," Alastar agreed, and stepped into the lift.\par
\par
     With the second commander gone, Alysha walked to the railing and looked over the first level of the bridge. The walls were lined with interfaces into the Stardancer's powerful computers. A glimpse of fire-red hair revealed Taylitha, wandering through the quiet order to the stairs. The woman ascended.\par
\par
     "You send Alastar down yet?"\par
\par
     "Just now," Alysha said as Taylitha joined her.\par
\par
     Taylitha nodded. "There's a lot of excitement down there, and some disappointment."\par
\par
     "Disappointment?"\par
\par
     "That we can't seem to find any signs of the people responsible for the possible ruins." Taylitha's ears canted sideways. "It's going to take us a day to scan the entire planet of the surface at the same resolution, though. Maybe we'll find something on the other side."\par
\par
     "Maybe," Alysha said.\par
\par
     Taylitha glanced at her. "What do you think, sir?"\par
\par
     "I don't know what to think," Alysha said, grinning. "That's most of the fun."\par
\par
     A chime drew both their gazes to the planet in the main display. A red dot appeared on its surface, then telescoped out to a small viseo of the obelisk, a few figures walking in front of it. Beneath the rectangle of blue, gray and sand, a number started counting upward; a second appeared beneath it, and a third, and two lines darted from the world. One connected to a swiftly drawn Well relay, and another to an equally quick-to-appear icon of the Stardancer in orbit.\par
\par
     A second rectangle opened with a topographical map of the area, with glowing blue dots moving across it.\par
\par
     "Following procedure to the letter, I see," Taylitha said. "Tagging everyone with an emitter? Pushing data to us and to . . . what, one of the central databases in the Core?"\par
\par
     "With the packets twinning as soon as they hit a redundant relay," Alysha said.\par
\par
     As more numbers appeared to indicate enough viseo recorders had been set up to start interleaving the 3deo feed the solidigraphic projectors most commonly used throughout the Stardancer, Taylitha glanced over at Alysha. "You want to go down there, don't you."\par
\par
     Alysha watched a separate column pop up on the display as several of the people on the surface applied themselves to getting viseo stills of the area for archeological record-keeping. She chuckled. "Whatever gave you that notion?"\par
\par
     "Maybe it was the way you're staring at the data. Like you want to eat it," Taylitha said, tapping her fingers against her folded arms.\par
\par
     "At very least I probably wouldn't have to duel to settle the fate of the planet," Alysha said, grinning.\par
\par
     Taylitha threw up her hands.\par
\par
     The emitters next to the captain's chair chimed, and Alysha said, "Display."\par
\par
     Alastar's body appeared next to the chair. "We're not reading any errors on this end, Captain." A pause, and another line connected the surface with the ship on the display. "And the redundant transceiver just went online. Ship-side confirms normal transmission error rate. Both transceivers are sending, standard encryption. There appear to be no problems with the simultaneous transmission to the data libraries in the Core. All crew-member transceivers have been activated, and all equipment has been tagged. The portable Pad station is in place and operating within normal tolerances. Viseo/3deo recorders are all online. Set-up procedure is complete."\par
\par
     "Noted," Alysha said, waited for the chime, "And logged. Proceed with your investigation, Second Commander."\par
\par
     "Aye, sir. Continuous updates as we take our readings. Virgil out."\par
\par
     "It's not customary for captains to poke around on unexplored planets," Taylitha said.\par
\par
     Alysha sat in the center chair, crossing her legs. "Nor is it against regulation." Taylitha's exasperated look elicited a chuckle. "You don't see me running to the Pad room this instant, do you? I won't go down until the survey team's had a chance to scout the area. But I will go down at some point."\par
\par
     "After I look around."\par
\par
     Alysha's brow lifted.\par
\par
     "And if I say it's not dangerous." Taylitha's tail lashed. "I'd prefer we didn't have to get a new captain so soon."\par
\par
     Alysha smiled, but her eyes were already lifting to the steady stream of data rolling down the side of the main display.\par
\par
