     Then smothering. The strands across her wrist squeezed, then released the pressure and tickled her with their stroking. Some find the dark smothering.\par
\par
     Who could ever find such vastness smothering? Alysha thought in wonderment. She rested her hand in the grip of the larger digits on the end of the tentacle, then floated on her back and rested her eyes on the stars.\par
\par
     Then the vastness.\par
\par
     And there was truth in that, for the vastness of the sky could be fearsome. A blue sky could conceal from the determined the implied space that surrounded a planet. But when darkness fell and the stars crept out to speckle the firmament, the near infinity of space could not be avoided.\par
\par
     That is why I love the stars, Alysha told the tentacle wistfully. Not because I want to know what's hiding behind every corner, but because it's comforting to know there will always be a place to go. You don't need to know all that is possible to be glad that you won't run out of possibilities before you die.\par
\par
     The waves thought it curious that she took comfort in the fact that the universe was larger than she.\par
\par
     Alysha thought it equally curious to deny the fact, and distasteful to fear it. There will always be something bigger than you, something better. Someone brighter. Something more glorious. Then you have the joy of knowing there are other wonderful things besides you. You will never run out of joy.\par
\par
     Another tentacle surfaced near her, and then the octopus's head rose over the water. Its gleaming orange eye seemed connected somehow to the redder stars. Alysha twisted onto her stomach and paddled, looking at the eye.\par
\par
     "Do you know such things?" she asked. "Do you know pleasure at the vastness of the universe?"\par
\par
     Pleasure, yes. Did she also know the bittersweetness of the universe's turning?\par
\par
     Alysha frowned, dipping one arm further under water. "Say again?" she murmured, hoping having more of herself submerged would help.\par
\par
     All things have two sides. You know joy. Sorrow?\par
\par
     The sorrow had a flavor as well as a smell, one she'd associated with the word 'bittersweet' in the first sending but seemed more complex in the second. In her mind, she saw a larger fish devouring a smaller one.\par
\par
     The inevitable? No, that wasn't right either. Alysha closed her eyes. More like the order of things. The pattern. Things die. People die. You ask if I see the sorrows of the same vastness that encompasses the joy of people's accomplishments?\par
\par
     The waves agreed. The octopus's unwavering eye remained on her.\par
\par
     "I do," Alysha said. She thought of a woman with wings, a child with too-knowing eyes and grimaced. She didn't always like it, though.\par
\par
     You don't always let it stand unchallenged.\par
\par
     No, Alysha said. She twirled onto her back again and drifted beside the tentacle. Sometimes it is my work to put myself between the inevitable and the innocent.\par
\par
     The innocent only?\par
\par
     With chagrin, Alysha thought, Well, the needy.\par
\par
     And if they do not want to be turned from their end in the vastness?\par
     Alysha's ears flipped backward, their tips into the ocean's weight. Then I have no leave to enter, do I?\par
\par
     Do you?\par
\par
     She tried to remember all the times she'd ever intervened and couldn't. Had she always bowed to the wishes of those she sought to help? Had she always even known their wishes? And yet, the principle felt true to her, true enough that it had seen her through the most difficult, most traumatic years of her life, when every day had been horror and anger. She had saved Rispa and Angel, but she'd left the Harem Rose to the life she had chosen.\par
\par
     No, I do not, Alysha said. I hold out my hand only when help is asked for. One man's hell is another man's paradise.\par
\par
     Rispa? Angel?\par
\par
     Alysha floated, holding serenity as close as she could. That is a long telling, and not a pleasant one.\par
\par
     It sounds similar to ours.\par
\par
     From the wash of the waves, Alysha felt more in 'similarity' than she would expect from the word: not a similarity in the surface of the tale, but a similarity in its emotional resonance. Sorrow is part of the vastness, she said. Not quite comforting, nor commiserating, but something between the two.\par
\par
     Sorrow has been part of our vastness, particularly. But we did not expect small ones who could hold that with equanimity.\par
\par
     Small ones? Alysha thought.\par
\par
     An image of thin creatures with four arms, long tails and wedge-shaped heads with horns appeared: the creatures from the murals. Small ones. A pause, then, and sorrowfully: our small ones, long taken by their own smallness.\par
