     Taylitha walked into the Medplex and dodged as a woman with a shock staff sprinted past. Flattening herself against the window-wall, the first commander craned her head around the corner into the ward. Laelkii was under one of the beds, tail lashing in frustration; around her, three women wearing light armor vests were attempting to hem in the alien--\par
\par
     --the alien! Awake and apparently spry, straddling the bed with a foot on either of the haloarch emitters. He had uncoiled his full length, both sets of arms spread wide and fingers curved like claws. He hissed at the approaching women with their shock staffs and net. The officer who'd dove past Taylitha joined them, trying to close the semi-circle and push the alien against the wall.\par
\par
     "Would you hurry up and get him back onto that bed?" Laelkii said testily.\par
\par
     "We're trying, doctor," one of the women answered without taking her eyes off their quarry. One of the shock staffs lowered toward the creature's tail, and Taylitha held her breath.\par
\par
     With a snarl, the alien leapt over their heads and landed awkwardly on one foot. He twisted around before they could grab him and bounded for Alastar's bed.\par
\par
     "Stop him!" Laelkii shouted, and the women jumped to follow.\par
\par
     "WAIT!" Taylitha cried, running from the entrance and skidding to a halt over Alastar. She flung her arms over the unconscious woman, her body supported a foot off 'Star's chest by the invisible haloarch.\par
\par
     The alien stopped abruptly on the other side of Alastar. He looked at her, cocking his head to one side like a bird.\par
\par
     "Remember me?" Taylitha asked breathlessly. She licked her lips, caught her breath, and continued more calmly. "I'm sure you do, since I haven't bathed or changed since you saw me last. I probably still stink the same, even."\par
\par
     He leaned toward her, and though Taylitha couldn't read his expression his body had a tentative, almost skeptical tension, as if he was ready to bolt.\par
\par
     Taylitha pointed to Alastar. "She's sick. You can't climb on her. See? She's unconscious."\par
\par
     The creature glanced at Alastar, then paused, brow knitting. It took a step closer and leaned over her.\par
\par
     "Taylitha," Laelkii said in a low, urgent voice.\par
\par
     "It's okay," Taylitha said. "He's just looking." She rolled her lip between her teeth as the alien's face drew closer to Alastar's. She almost said something when he sniffed at her mouth and nose, but held her peace. The creature's body had relaxed, grown just slack enough for her to believe he meant no harm.\par
\par
     "Do you know what's wrong with her? Do you recognize it?" Taylitha asked softly. "If we figure out how to talk to you . . . will you tell us how to help her?"\par
\par
     The creature completed his inspection and lifted his face to look at Taylitha askance. He peeped.\par
\par
     Taylitha sighed and propped up her face with a hand. The haloarch's field tickled her elbow, but she leaned on it anyway.\par
\par
     "Peep? What does that mean?" Laelkii asked. She crawled out of her refuge and walked a wide circle around Alastar's bed to stand next to Taylitha.\par
\par
     "Your guess is as good as mine," Taylitha said. To the security team, she said, "I think we'll only need one of you, just in case. The rest of you can go."\par
\par
     "Aye, sir."\par
\par
     The creature hissed and stepped toward the wall as the team filed out, but when they didn't return, he continued looking at Alastar.\par
\par
     "So have you learned anything about him?" Taylitha asked.\par
\par
     Laelkii said, "Muscloskeletal system isn't amazingly dissimilar from ours. And thought I couldn't tell you what most of them do yet, he does seem to have organs in most of the right places. I was looking at his brain when he sprang out of bed." She looked with chagrin at her uniform sleeve, nearly in two pieces. "Used my arm as a lever on the way up. Thank sun and stars for fur or I'd have a handful of pretty welts to go with the new uniform order. He's got talons."\par
\par
     "Is he a he?" Taylitha asked.\par
\par
     "I can't tell either way," Laelkii said. "I found no sexual characteristics, primary or secondary. He has spaces that look like they're meant to accommodate what I assume are sexual organs, but the tissue there isn't differentiated. My best guess is he's immature."\par
\par
     "Or he buds, like a Flitzbe," Taylitha said, and finally laughed. The sound startled the alien into staring at her intently.\par
\par
     "Sorry," Taylitha said to him, then leaned to the wall and touched one of the panels on it. The computer's soft chime startled the alien a second time, and as Taylitha leaned toward the panel in weariness he arched his body over the bed to peer at the panel.\par
\par
     "Taylitha to obelisk site."\par
\par
     "McCrearty here."\par
\par
     "Are all the staff linguists on site?"\par
\par
     "Aye, sir. Three on night shift, four on day."\par
\par
     "Send a couple of them up here." Taylitha looked at the alien. "Ask for the ones who are good at singing or whistling."\par
\par
     The woman sounded puzzled, but she answered, "Aye, sir. I'll have them up immediately."\par
\par
     "Thanks. Basil out." Taylitha turned to Laelkii. "Did you figure out what was wrong with him, anyway?"\par
\par
     "He was dehydrated and malnourished," Laelkii said. "At least, that's as far as I got. I fed him some simple sugars in solution."\par
\par
     "Right," Taylitha said, turning to look at the alien. He straightened and returned her regard. "Well, we can't talk to him until the linguists arrive--"\par
\par
     "--arrive and figure out how he talks," Laelkii said. "That might take a while."\par
\par
     "And in the mean, we need to do something with him. Suggestions?"\par
\par
     "You could shower and sleep," Laelkii said, studying the creature with too distinct an air of innocence.\par
\par
     Taylitha scowled at her. "I meant with the alien. I know I need a shower and a nap, and that's what I was about to do when Admiral what's-his-name interrupted me and demanded to know what Alysha had done with his extra-special mail. As if I knew!"\par
\par
     "What did you tell him?" Laelkii asked, ears perked with interest.\par
\par
     "That I'd get back to him," Taylitha said. "But that's neither here nor there. About the alien!"\par
\par
     "Right," Laelkii said. "I can put him in an isolation ward, but I can't imagine that being very pleasant for him. On the other hand, leaving him in a room is probably a bad idea too. He might get in trouble with most of the appliances. The brig's definitely depressing . . . "\par
\par
     Taylitha tried to think around her developing headache. "The gardens. Let's put him in the gardens."\par
\par
     Laelkii's ears sagged. "Hydroponics is pretty big. He could get lost in there."\par
\par
     "We'll cordon off an area. Do you have any better suggestions?"\par
\par
     Laelkii shook her head. "Just let me assign a healer with the security team. I'm not sure if he's in the best of health yet. He might have problems I haven't had time to identify, and I could use additional data."\par
\par
     "Right," Taylitha said, before turning to the woman left by the security team.\par
