     "Well, at least it's a beautiful night," Darya said with a yawn. She wiggled her bare toes in the sand and turned from her appraisal of the horizon to find Steffis sliding a knife into a belt sheath and picking up a shock staff. "Angels on the field, Steffis! Are we heading into a war zone?"\par
\par
     The striped engineer tied her hair back into a tail and looked at Darya with a grim face. "We already lost one officer in those caverns. I'm not letting another one go without a fight."\par
\par
     "That's presuming the captain's lost," Darya said. "Somehow she doesn't strike me as the type."\par
\par
     "Yeah, well, neither did Commander Virgil," Steffis said. "A more level-headed woman I haven't met. So better safe, right, Lieutenant Darteriov?"\par
\par
     "Riiight," Darya said. "And it's Darya. I don't know any homeworld-bred Harat-Shar who go by their train names."\par
\par
     "Darya, then," Steffis said. She checked her kit one more time, then glanced over her shoulder at the camp. Her second waved to her casually. "Shall we?"\par
\par
     "To the caves," Darya said cheerfully, and set off across the sands. "So what's your first name, Lieutenant?"\par
\par
     "Julien," Steffis said warily.\par
\par
     Darya squinted. "Like the man's name?"\par
\par
     "No, that's Julian with an 'a'."\par
\par
     "Oh. Then like the vegetables?"\par
\par
     "No, that's julienne, accent on the second syllable. My name is Julien. Julien Steffis."\par
\par
     "So can I call you Julien?" Darya asked. "All this rank and surname bit is so confining."\par
\par
     Steffis eyed her. "I've heard about you Harat-Shar."\par
\par
     "Only heard?" Darya said with a grin.\par
\par
     "Well, there aren't any Harat-Shar in engineering," Steffis said.\par
\par
     "--And let me guess, you don't get out much," Darya said. She chuckled at Steffis's glower. "Oh, I'm not going to tease you about it. Much. Unless it'll get you to relax." She glanced at Steffis's white-knuckled grip on the shock staff.\par
\par
     "I'll relax once the captain's checked in," Steffis said, tail lashing.\par
\par
     "But according to the first commander, if she's not there she's already gone back to the 'Dancer."\par
\par
     Steffis's ears flattened. "Or maybe she's vanished somewhere and Commander Basil doesn't want us to know."\par
\par
     "Now that's paranoia," Darya said. "I like gossip about superior officers as much as the next girl, but let's not borrow trouble unless it's fun."\par
\par
     When Steffis didn't reply, Darya sighed and let the conversation founder. The purple of the night sky was too new and too beautiful to waste if the engineer wasn't going to loosen up enough for interesting talk. The woman hadn't even changed into a more comfortable uniform. Almost every other person working downstairs had defaulted to sleeveless blouses and khakis and most were barefoot. Steffis was still in full shipboard regalia, from her boots to her stiff collared tunic.\par
\par
     They'd walked all the way to the end of the seaward march and turned toward the north when Steffis finally broke the silence. "Is that . . . water?"\par
\par
     Darya squinted toward the caves. "I thought that was the shore."\par
\par
     "It's reflecting the light, though. And moving. Stars, I think the cave's flooded!" Steffis launched from the sands and sprinted for the cave, leaving Darya to scramble after. The pantherine arrived on Steffis's heels to find the ocean's waves slapping into the cave, foaming starlit silver at its rocky edges. Darya scanned the sands, wondering what had inspired the tide to rise so high, and caught a glint of light on skin.\par
\par
     "The captain!" Steffis exclaimed, lunging for the cave.\par
\par
     "Wait!" Darya shouted, grabbing for Steffis's arm and missing.\par
\par
     A giant tentacle whipped up before them and slammed into the sand in front of the engineer, who skidded to a halt with a squawk and leapt in the opposite direction, landing in Darya's arms.\par
\par
     "Sun-and-stars-and-oh-gods-what-is-that!"\par
\par
     Darya pulled Steffis away and stepped back herself as a giant eye surfaced from the waters by the sand and an octopus crawled toward them. One tentacle extended into the cave's mouth as the water sloshed around it. The others wove along the strand as the creature eased to a halt, barring the way to the cavern.\par
\par
     "Whatever it is, it's between us and the cave," Darya said.\par
\par
     Steffis stared, horrified. "Stars, the captain!"\par
\par
     "She might not even be in there," Darya said. "Let's find out first, okay?" She clicked her tongue in the back of her mouth to activate her telegem, which chimed in her rounded black ear.\par
\par
     "Computer, is the captain aboard?"\par
\par
     A pause, then, "Captain Forrest is not on the Stardancer."\par
\par
     Steffis shot her a wild glance, and Darya restrained her urge to shake the engineer. Or the computer. Either would do, though the one would be easier than the other. "Are you sure she's just not available?"\par
\par
     "The captain is not aboard the ship."\par
\par
     "Fine," Darya said, watching the octopus. It hadn't moved. "She must be on planet then."\par
\par
     "The captain is not on the planet."\par
\par
     Darya paused. "Are you sure?"\par
\par
     "The captain is not on the planet."\par
\par
     "Or on the ship."\par
\par
     "The captain is not on the ship."\par
\par
     Steffis grabbed Darya's wrist. "Look, she's in the cave! I'm telling you! It's the only place the computer couldn't find her telegem!"\par
\par
     "Well, maybe she's in some other part of the network," Darya said. "We mapped a whole lot of tunnels next to Alastar's cave."\par
\par
     "If she's in some other part of it, why is this animal sitting on the entrance? Maybe it's decided that it's found its next meal!"\par
\par
     "I don't think octopuses eat Pelted," Darya said.\par
\par
     "That's not an octopus," Steffis said, irritated. "It has more than eight legs, and it's on an alien planet. Who knows what it eats?"\par
\par
     "Good point," Darya said. "At least it doesn't seem hostile."\par
\par
     "To us!" Steffis said.\par
\par
     "To us," Darya allowed. She sighed. "What I know about octopuses--"\par
\par
     "--aliens," Steffis said, reaching up to the end of her shock staff.\par
\par
     "--aliens," Darya said without skipping a beat, "could fit into a claw paring. We should get someone to help us remove the octo--alien . . . Julien, what are you doing?"\par
\par
     The engineer's hand unlocked the barrier cap on the end of the shock staff and slid it down to the collar, exposing the spear tip. "I am going to move that animal."\par
\par
     Darya grabbed the shock staff. "You can't just go knifing it! What if it's intelligent?"\par
\par
     "It's an animal!"\par
\par
     "Even if it is an animal," Darya said. "It's a very big animal. Animals get violent when injured. And what if it's brooding? Injured mother animals are even worse. We can't just stick a spear in it and hope it'll die."\par
\par
     "So what do you propose we do?" Steffis asked. "Let it eat the captain?"\par
\par
     "If the captain is in there, and if she's in danger, we'll save her," Darya said firmly. "But not by inciting something twice our size to violence. Why don't we find someone who knows something about octopi and ask about their behavior?"\par
\par
     "Octopuses," Steffis said. "And it's not an octopus. Count the arms." She sighed. "Fine. Let's wake up one of the biologists. But if it makes one move toward the cave . . . "\par
\par
     Darya decided that calling attention back to the arm already inside the cave was a bad idea and clicked her way back to the on-board computer. "Biology lab."\par
\par
     After a few moments, a sharp contralto answered. "Biology lab, this is Tara Demier. How can I help you?"\par
\par
     "This is Lieutenant Darya, on the surface of the planet. We have a minor emergency, and we're looking for a marine biologist. Is anyone available?"\par
\par
     "A minor emergency?" The voice sounded concerned. "What kind of minor emergency?"\par
\par
     "Some pesky sealife," Darya said, ignoring Steffis's pained expression. "We could really use some help on how to remove a critter that doesn't want to be removed. Could you get me someone?"\par
\par
     "Hang on," the voice said. "We've only got a couple marine biologists, but one of them should be awake. She's a night-owl. Okay . . . patching you through." A moment later, a husky alto answered. "This is Ensign Perralta. How can I help?"\par
\par
     Steffis had gone back to watching the creature. Darya tried to split her attention between the engineer and the alien. "This is Lieutenant Darya. Sorry to bother you, ensign, but you wouldn't happen to know anything about octopuses, would you?"\par
\par
     "Octopuses? The Terran sea creature? A little, though that wasn't my planet of study and it's been a while since I've crammed for exams. Hang on, let me page through some references . . . why the interest, sir?"\par
\par
     "Darya will do. We have an octopus-like creature on the shore that's in our way, ensign. We want to know why it's in our way and how we can suggest that it move."\par
\par
     "If you have a net you should be able to pick it up and move it without hurting it."\par
\par
     Darya tried not to laugh. "I'm not sure we have a net that could do the job. Do you think prodding it with a shock staff would get it to move?"\par
\par
     "If you want to move it by killing it, maybe," Perralta said. "How big is this thing, Darya?"\par
\par
     "Oh... hmmm. I'd say about seven feet tall?" Darya squinted. "The arms look about twice that. Maybe a little under."\par
\par
     "What! And that's in your way?"\par
\par
     "Absolutely," Darya said.\par
\par
     "This I've got to see. I'll be right down!"\par
\par
     "Err, ensign--" But the telegem cheeped a closed connection and Darya covered her eyes.\par
\par
     "So what did she say?" Steffis asked.\par
\par
     "She's on her way to assess the situation," Darya said. "At least the creature's not moving."\par
\par
     Steffis flexed her hand on the shock spear.\par
