\par
     Your small ones are gone, Alysha thought, holding onto the tentacle. What happened? How did their smallness hurt them?\par
\par
     They did not like the order of things. The wavelets gently brushed her fur. They did not like how we responded to the order of things. Like you, they preferred to put themselves between others and what awaited them.\par
\par
     You preferred the natural order, Alysha said.\par
\par
     Yes.\par
\par
     The response held so much more than a simple affirmative that Alysha let herself drift in the water in silence, trying to absorb it. The bittersweetness. The regret. Currents of pain and argument. A 'yes' that held several 'no's.\par
\par
     You handle this well, little one.\par
\par
     This? Alysha asked. The talking?\par
\par
     The talking. Our small ones could not sense the layers in the waters. And the other small one, who came just before you did . . . she did not have your discipline.\par
\par
     Alysha's muscles tightened. "The other small one? Alastar?"\par
\par
     The waters seemed warm with the waves that followed, as if the alien was trying to draw the images from her mind. Alysha held on to her mental picture of Alastar, still awake and healthy.\par
\par
     Yes, that one. She could not keep the layers separate from her own mind's.\par
\par
     Alysha's fingers flexed on the arm. I see. And I can.\par
\par
     So it seems.\par
\par
     She will wake?\par
\par
     One presumes. For all our senses, even we cannot push so much into the water that it does not eventually fade. Everything fades.\par
\par
     "I see," Alysha murmured aloud. Some of her worry for Alastar eased. "So your small ones disagreed with some of your choices. What choice did you make that they disliked?"\par
\par
     They do not understand our way of life. It was very different. There is only so much space for us here, in these oceans.\par
\par
     Alysha glanced at the vast glittering waters.\par
\par
     By your thoughts, you seem to think elsewise. But not physical space.\par
\par
     Flashes of sea bottoms, encrusted with coral. Creatures flooded on tides. Memories of biology books discussing the balance of species needed for the health of an ecosystem. Alysha touched a hand to her forehead and closed her eyes, re-ordering the memories. An issue of the order of things, she said at last. Too many of you cause an imbalance in the lives of others?\par
\par
     Just so.\par
\par
     And you . . . choose not to overpopulate.\par
\par
     It was not a difficult decision. Not long after the egg-layers of our species drop their young, they die. If they withhold their eggs, they live longer lives.\par
\par
     Alysha glanced at the orange eye. "There's more to this than the decision not to reproduce."\par
\par
     Yes. The destroyers.\par
\par
     A flash of black menace with glowing yellow eyes and bloodstained teeth.\par
\par
     "The leviathans from the cave paintings," Alysha murmured.\par
\par
     Predators, the octopus said. They come for us. Age makes us slow. Our egg-layers died quickly.\par
\par
     Alysha watched teeth rip into mottled flesh and controlled her wince. Looking past the first images, she felt the subtext on the waters. "You do not resist."\par
\par
     It is part of the natural order of things.\par
\par
     If it's part of the natural order of things, then you wouldn't have to worry about not reproducing, Alysha said. The destroyers would keep your numbers down.\par
\par
     So the small ones said. They urged us to fight the destroyers. To let them help us destroy them. But the destroyers are part of the order of things. Their absence would rip apart this tapestry.\par
\par
     Just as yours would, Alysha said.\par
\par
     The tentacle curled around her waist, furling the soft membrane around her hips and legs, almost an embrace. Just so. But we had not counted on the effect of the small ones on the egg-layers. The small ones convinced the egg-layers to live, rather than to lay.\par
\par
     Alysha paused. "Some would say that living is preferable to dying," she said.\par
\par
     But that was not the question. The question was which was of more value: long life, or the pleasure of bearing young. Have you born young?\par
\par
     No, Alysha said.\par
\par
     Do you plan to?\par
\par
     Alysha's ears flattened. "I hadn't really thought about it," she said. "Among my kind, child-rearing is a commitment of many years and social custom requires you engage a suitable mate to help you with the duty."\par
\par
     Very different. Almost too different . . . can you still understand how child-bearing can be a value of its own?\par
\par
     I think, Alysha said. She thought of Laelkii. At very least, most of the mothers I've known would not trade their children for longer lives.\par
\par
     Part of being one of us is knowing that bearing young is a value greater than a long but fruitless life. The small ones could not understand that for us the chain of life is worth more than an individual's contribution to it.\par
\par
     Sudden understanding gripped her mind. "So they wanted you to not reproduce as much to save your lives, and in order to keep your numbers high to fight the leviathans off. Is that it?"\par
\par
