     Taylitha jumped awake, head swimming with admirals, aliens and images of Alysha and Alastar in identical, dripping comas. She shuddered in her blankets, then flopped over and stared at the glowing chronolog floating over her workspace. Barely after 6 mark. She'd only been asleep an hour and a half or so. Maybe two.\par
\par
     "An in space," she said with a groan. "The report. Alysha!" She whistled open a channel.\par
\par
     "Computer, patch me to Steffis."\par
\par
     Silence. Taylitha used it to wonder how embarrassed she should be if in the intervening hours Steffis had left her a message that she'd failed to check before frantically calling. She'd decided on 'not embarrassed, but not demanding'--after all, they were supposed to have called her fifteen minutes after her first request, and they hadn't--when the computer answered.\par
\par
     "Lieutenant Steffis is not responding."\par
\par
     Taylitha dropped the blanket from enervated hands. "Excuse me?"\par
\par
     "Lieutenant Steffis is n--"\par
\par
     "Never mind that," Taylitha said, waving a clawed hand in the dark. "Get me Darya."\par
\par
     "Lieutenant Darteriov is not responding."\par
\par
     Taylitha gaped. "I guess it's too much to ask for the captain," she said in a small voice.\par
\par
     "Alysha Forrest is not available."\par
\par
     Taylitha buried her face in her hands. "Oh God in the sunlit heavens, oh God. No. Can't panic. Computer, can you locate Darya or Steffis?"\par
\par
     "The transceivers in Lieutenant Steffis's and Lieutenant Darteriov's telegems are not responding."\par
\par
     What had they said last time she'd talked to them? They'd located the captain in the caves, but there was an octopus in the way and they'd called a biologist to investigate. "Basil to the Biology Lab."\par
\par
     "Tara Demier here. How can I help you sir?"\par
\par
     "Good morning. Was a biologist sent downstairs earlier, Tara?" Taylitha asked.\par
\par
     "Ah, yes. Ensign Perralta. She should still be on planet. Lieutenant Darya called her down."\par
\par
     "That's all I needed to know," Taylitha said. "Thanks." She whistled that closed, then took a long breath and said, "Computer. Ensign Perralta please."\par
\par
     "Ensign Perralta is not respo--"\par
\par
     "--onding, right," Taylitha said. Had they even found the captain before succumbing to whatever it was that lurked in that cave? An unbidden image flashed before her eyes: an angry yellow eye. She shuddered. What could she do? Send more people after them? What if they got lost as well?\par
\par
     Darya, Steffis, the biologist . . . surely they were capable, just like the captain. Still, she couldn't trust that they'd come back on their own. Taylitha whistled the computer to the main channel being used for command traffic for the digs.\par
\par
     "This is Basil."\par
\par
     Two "Good mornings," greeted her, the two officers currently overseeing both sites. No doubt several score more were listening in, though they were barred from adding to the traffic.\par
\par
     "If any of you see Steffis, tell her I'm looking for her," Taylitha said, doing her best to sound like a slightly irascible, underslept commander and not a frantically worried one left in command of the starship. "She and Darya had an assignment last night and they seem to have gotten lost."\par
\par
     A long pause, then one of the women said, "We'll keep an ear out for them, Commander."\par
\par
     "Thanks," Taylitha said, and added for good measure, "I can't imagine what they could be doing. Basil out."\par
\par
     The line chimed its close, leaving what she hoped was harmless and raunchy speculation about what could have distracted someone who'd gone off alone with a Harat-Shar from making a report. Now the women downstairs would be looking for the missing people without, Taylitha hoped, too much alarm.\par
\par
     She had problems of her own . . . like getting out the report she promised to Exploration on Alysha's behalf, or finding a plausible excuse. Report-writing was nothing new to Taylitha, but linguistics wasn't her specialty. If the linguists hadn't done something with the native in the hours Taylitha had been fretting over Alysha's safety, there was no way she'd be able to fake a report, no matter how much practice she'd had writing sweet nothings for Personnel.\par
\par
     Of course, she could say that Alysha was still in negotiations with the natives, and that the captain had asked Taylitha to prepare a preliminary report in her absence. Never mind that it was a lie: if Alysha had been on board, she surely would have been doing just that, closeted with the native until they could understand one another. In such a circumstance, Alysha would of course ask for her aid in the paperwork. Taylitha was good with paperwork. The excuse would fly. She just had to come up with enough useful information to write something.\par
\par
     So, to the Medplex. Taylitha stepped out of bed and stumbled, catching herself on the edge of the night-table. She stared at her trembling leg and then dropped her ears in chagrin. Of course, she'd barely slept and not well. She'd have to have coffee. Didn't the captain have coffee every morning? Taylitha tried to remember ever seeing Alysha exhausted and failed. With a sigh, she tied her hair back and got dressed, moving slowly.\par
\par
     The Medplex was extremely quiet when Taylitha entered, stopping uncertainly by the fish tanks in the antechamber. Before she could step through to triage, a slim woman took her by the arm and drew her back into the antechamber.\par
