\par
     "None," the woman said. Her uniform had a lieutenant's stripe, her badge the medical division's aquamarine dots. According to the badge, she was Jewel Thandry. "Or so close to none it might as well be none. We got him inside and he just stopped communicating. No whistles, no cheeping, nothing. The linguists can't work without data. I can't work without stimulus. It's a mess."\par
\par
     "So he's just . . . sleeping?" Taylitha asked.\par
\par
     Thandry shook her head. "No. Catatonic, or he was for the first few hours. He just curled up in a ball and didn't move or respond to anything. I was monitoring his vitals, but I didn't see anything obviously wrong. Then a little while ago, he made for the tree and he's been there since."\par
\par
     "Catatonic?" Taylitha asked.\par
\par
     "Or close," Thandry said. She sighed and rubbed the back of her neck. "It's been a long night."\par
\par
     "It's safe to go in, I assume," Taylitha said.\par
\par
     "Be my guest, sir."\par
\par
     Taylitha nodded and let herself into the enclosure. She smiled at the Karaka'An and looked up into the tree. The native had curled himself into a rough ball and wedged himself between trunk and branch with his back to the room, only his tail hanging down.\par
\par
     "You seemed a little feistier earlier," Taylitha said to the tail. "What happened? Do you not like the gardens?"\par
\par
     The native lifted his head and bent it at a truly astonishing angle to peer over his multiple sets of shoulder blades. Beside Taylitha, the Karaka'An fumbled at her data tablet.\par
\par
     "Peep?"\par
\par
