#38\par
\par
 \par
\par
     The braided cord led Alysha back into the friendly purple waters with their gilded arabesques, past a surprising school of flat turquoise fish expressed against the backdrop as a few swatches of color and a defining silver edge here and there. They swerved around her and fled, leaving a blooming cloud of lavender bubbles that tickled on their way toward the surface. She glided past them, one hand on the plait near her waist.\par
\par
     Her path took her out of the purple waters and into a wash of bright gold, so bright it faded the defining lines to something closer to the color of bronze. Squinting against the glare, Alysha waded deeper into what became a glittering cave. She heard laughter, rich and melodious, and followed it into a den stacked with pillows. There were bodies in it, bright purple bodies, but so intertwined Alysha couldn't tell how many there were.\par
\par
     She had a good idea what they were doing, though.\par
\par
     "Lieutenant?"\par
\par
     It took several giggles and purrs for Darya to extricate herself from her fellows. She brushed off her purple fur and said, "Yes, Captain?"\par
\par
     "Where's Rennagan? And did you find Steffis?"\par
\par
     "Felsha's looking for Julien. My dream came and found me so I thought I'd enjoy it," Darya said.\par
\par
     Alysha glanced at the dream, which had resolved into a patchwork room of sunlight and sisters with purple fur. Everything was soft: carpets on the floors, the furniture, plush pillows, rounded curves. "It looks worth enjoying," she said.\par
\par
     "Stay, Captain?"\par
\par
     Alysha chuckled. "Not until I find out what happened to Steffis. But thank you for the invitation."\par
\par
     "Okay," Darya said and slipped back into the pile. Alysha watched their languorous cuddle for a few moments, struggling to ignore the sudden sense that trouble awaited them all. She turned to go, realized her waist was still yoked to something.\par
\par
     "Darya?"\par
\par
     "Mmmmm . . . yes, sir?"\par
\par
     "What did you anchor this rope to?"\par
\par
     "Me," Darya said. The tip of her tail flicked into view, wearing a jeweled ring. Alysha nodded. "I'll be back, Lieutenant."\par
\par
     The gold waters faded as Alysha swam out of them into tunnels that seemed the color of faded bronze filigreed with golden tendrils. Perhaps Darya's mind was feeding their entire venture, from the art style that looked like her remembered paintings to the weakness of the vision where it intersected other people's nightmares. She wondered if the octopi had arranged it so based on Darya's being the first to decide in favor of the share-walk.\par
\par
     The caves that adjoined Darya's dream remained solid. Alysha hoped for an easy solution and called, "Steffis?"\par
\par
     "Here, sir!"\par
\par
     Small mercies . . . the woman wasn't far. Alysha headed that way, turned a corner and ended up floating above a pit. Beneath her feet Steffis was barely visible, the crown of her head glowing in the darkness of the obliette.\par
\par
     "Lieutenant?" Alysha asked, perplexed.\par
\par
     "Captain! Finally. I've been looking for a way out of here. I wasn't looking forward to pulling the pins out of my collar and using them as pitons."\par
\par
     "We're floating in water, Lieutenant."\par
\par
     "You may be floating in water, Captain, but I'm in the dark down here and there's not a drop of water to be found. Could you throw me a rope?"\par
\par
     The forboding that had seeped into her spirit in Darya's room blossomed just a touch further. Alysha unwrapped the plait from around her waist and dropped it into the hole. It grew taut, and soon Steffis appeared on the wall, heading toward her. Alysha watched until the engineer crested the top of the pit, then she helped Steffis out.\par
\par
     "Thanks, Captain."\par
\par
     "Have you seen Rennagan, Lieutenant?"\par
\par
     "I haven't, sorry," Steffis said. "I was trying to get my bearings when this tunnel system appeared, and then I fell into that hole." She scratched her nose. "Funny thing. Reminds me of some of my dreams. I guess that makes sense given where we are."\par
\par
     "I'm going to find Rennagan," Alysha said. "I'd prefer it if we all remained in one place. That rope will lead you back to Darya."\par
\par
     Steffis grimaced. "Can't I wait elsewhere, sir?"\par
\par
     Alysha didn't reply.\par
\par
     With a sigh, Steffis said, "Yes, sir." She straightened her uniform tunic and began walking with determination back up the corridor. Her feet met the ground, boot-heels scraping stone.\par
\par
     Alysha watched until she vanished, then turned and swam out of the stone halls and onto a surprising beach at night. Two silvery moons glowed in the black sky, and the sands reflected their light, gray with glimmers of cool white. The rush of the surf broke against ten large bodies and the silhouettes of much smaller ones milling around each.\par
\par
     Felsha was sitting on a hill, hugging her knees with hands casually linked at her shins. Alysha joined her.\par
\par
     "We managed to get them back into the water," Felsha said.\par
\par
     Alysha looked again at the shore.\par
\par
     "And then, a week later, they were back," Felsha said. "We did it again. And again the following week. They didn't come back after that. For a while I wore a special shirt to school: Velvet Whale Watch, it said. A black shirt with a spiny cartoon whale on it the color of a roasted almond. Someone complained that they hadn't gotten the color right, that the whales were more a shiny gold in the right light. I was pretty proud." Felsha stroked the fur on her knee against the grain. "I guess this is the first thing I thought about when we fell asleep. Maybe we'll get Alien Octopi Watch shirts. Do you think?"\par
\par
     "I don't know," Alysha said, thinking of the natural order of things. "If we do, I somehow doubt they'll get the colors right."\par
\par
     "Maybe if they put some of those iridescent dyes," Felsha mused.\par
\par
     "It wouldn't be as beautiful as the real thing," Alysha said.\par
\par
     "Probably not," Felsha said. She stood up. "I guess I didn't do a great job of finding Steffis, wandering away and getting all caught up in my own memories."\par
\par
     "We're here to dream, Lieutenant," Alysha said, rising. She looked out over the great lumps stranded on the strand and felt the whisper of desperation, of wariness, of unease. "Does anyone know why they beached themselves?"\par
\par
     Felsha shook her head. "We assumed they got trapped by the currents. That's what the biologists said."\par
\par
     "Not a sapient species," Alysha said.\par
\par
     "No," Felsha said. "At least, no one said anything about them being self-aware."\par
\par
     And so they'd been shipped back to their waters. Had their second and third trips to the sands been unintentional . . . or had they been trying to die? The wind off the beach was cold.\par
\par
     "Let's get back to Darya," Alysha said. "Maybe once we're all together we'll be able to develop a plan."\par
\par
     They walked back toward the caves, the sand giving beneath their feet as they continued up the small mound.\par
\par
     "Strange how even in the dreams we're still around the ocean with caves and beaches," Felsha said.\par
\par
     "Wait until you see what Darya's made of it all," Alysha said with a chuckle.\par
\par
     "I'm sure it's opulent. She's shown me pictures from home, though, sir, and her train lives underground too. Not deeply... just beneath the surface, where it's cooler."\par
\par
     "I suppose that makes sense," Alysha said as they trudged into the cave. She could no longer float and wondered if that meant anything.\par
\par
     "Do you hear something, sir?"\par
\par
     Faintly, raised voices. Then a definite crash. Alysha broke into a run with Felsha at her heels. They ran all the way up the cavern, following the shouts, the wails, the sound of breaking glass and wood, until they found themselves at the arch into Darya's cozy room, which was no longer so cozy. Darya's sisters were huddled behind her, alternately spitting, hissing or wailing. Steffis and Darya stood on opposite ends of the room with the wreckage between them.\par
\par
     "What's going on here?" Alysha asked.\par
\par
     "She started it," Darya said. "She's got a knife!"\par
\par
     Steffis didn't bother to hide the blade in her hand. "I did not start it. Her sisters did by coming near me."\par
\par
     "They were just trying to include you so you wouldn't be so lonely!" Darya said.\par
\par
     "I was fine where I was," Steffis said. "If I'd wanted company, I would have asked!"\par
\par
     "Would you have?" Darya said, lip curling. "Or would you have just sat in the corner and tried to make a virtue out of your loneliness? Brave Julien Steffis who doesn't need anyone! Don't touch her, girls, she bites!"\par
\par
     "Enough!" Alysha said.\par
\par
     "I knew I shouldn't have come here," Steffis said to Alysha. "You should have had me wait somewhere else."\par
\par
     "I trusted that both of you could handle yourselves as responsible adults," Alysha said. "I don't think breaking furniture counts."\par
\par
     "They were -fondling- me," Steffis said. "You have no idea what it's like--"\par
\par
     "Enough," Alysha said, and this time the disapproval in her voice sharpened it so fine it cut through a few of the gold curls in the room. "Darya, I require your answer as a Fleet lieutenant trained to deal with the cultures of other worlds and people. Did your sisters cross a line?"\par
\par
     Darya's tail flicked once. "One of them petted the lieutenant's shoulder. Another stroked her knee. I have done the same to Felsha without causing her to throw a lamp at me."\par
\par
     "It's my knee. My shoulder. My personal space!" Steffis said. "I didn't give permission!"\par
\par
     "We didn't mean any harm!" Darya said.\par
\par
     "Steffis, why the lamp?"\par
\par
     "They were too close." Steffis's ears were flat to her head. "They were petting me. If I hadn't stopped them, they would have done something untoward."\par
\par
     "How can you say that!" Darya exclaimed, hands curled into fists. "Everyone knows ours is a culture of consent. We aren't turned on by people who don't want to play!"\par
\par
     "Right," Steffis said. "So that's why you tease and flirt with everyone whether they like that kind of thing or not."\par
\par
     "I see I made a mistake in assuming you would conduct yourselves in this place the way you would outside it," Alysha said. "But I'm not going to tolerate violence. Darya, I'm not entirely pleased you didn't instruct your sisters to stay clear of Lieutenant Steffis. Many people might assume your permissiveness is equivalent to stupidity, but I am not one of them. You know the lieutenant was unwilling and no amount of friendly invitation will change her mind."\par
\par
     Darya deflated. Alysha turned to Steffis, "But you, lieutenant... I would have thought better."\par
\par
     The engineer remained stiffly defiant, arms crossed over her breast.\par
\par
     "You're going back to the surface," Alysha said. "We don't solve our problems with our fists . . . or our knives. Find Perralta and go with her to the First Commander."\par
\par
     "It's not right, sir," Steffis said. "I have the right to protect myself."\par
\par
     "You have the right to action in proportion to the offense and the offenders," Alysha said. "Draw steel on a man who's about to throw you down and rape you . . . not on a group of Harat-Shariin girls who just want to help you relax."\par
\par
     "You would do the same--"\par
\par
     "I wouldn't," Alysha said, her voice hardening, "And I haven't. Get out of here, Lieutenant, before I make a note on your record for insubordination . . . subconsciously offered or not."\par
\par
     The faint outline of the tentacle appeared behind Steffis as if summoned; perhaps it had been. Steffis turned into it and became diffuse, a few strokes of copper and gold that still somehow conveyed her anger.\par
\par
     "If anyone needs relaxing, it's her," Darya said.\par
\par
     "Be quiet, Lieutenant," Alysha said. "I'm not all that pleased with you either right now."\par
\par
     The broken furniture remained, but tendrils of darkness began to creep onto the walls, blotting out the windows. The golden shadows in the room dulled to brass, then became completely black.\par
\par
     "I don't like this," Felsha said, standing near the door as the wallpaper next to her began to peel, a gaudy red velvet unevenly applied.\par
\par
     Neither did Alysha, though she recognized the place. She knew the ugly walls, the furniture, the tired yellow cast of the fake candlelight sconces. She knew the stink.\par
\par
     She knew that cursing Steffis for bringing the scene on would be unfair, but still she wanted to do it. This would have come to her anyway. From the moment she'd told her guide that she would allow no Tiells to bully the Alliance, she'd known somehow that she would have to explain herself, that it wouldn't be enough to simply say she knew when to save the afflicted and when to let them choose their own fates.\par
\par
     "Captain?" Darya asked, unease quelling her usual enthusiasm. "Where are we?"\par
\par
     "It's my turn," Alysha said.\par
\par
\par
#39\par
\par
 \par
\par
     "Taylitha? Are you awake?"\par
\par
     No, she was not awake. She was dreaming 'Star's insistent voice out of a deep desire for 'Star to be all right.\par
\par
     Someone shook her shoulder once. "Taylitha!" And then, "No, don't move!"\par
\par
     Taylitha opened her eyes and tried to think through a groggy haze. Someone's knees were next to her face. "Where am I?"\par
\par
     "On the floor in one of the Medplex waiting rooms," Alastar said. "You've been asleep a good three hours."\par
\par
     That explained the unforgiving aches on her shoulder and along her sides. The one on her hip hurt so much it felt like a bruise. "Can I wipe the sleep from my eyes, at least?"\par
\par
     "Yes. Just don't move your leg. The alien's asleep on it."\par
\par
     Which explained the sore knee. Taylitha glanced down at the wedge-shaped head and exclaimed, "An! Is he--"\par
\par
     "He's worse," Alastar said, ears falling. "The shift changed once and he didn't move, though he watched them. He seems to prefer First Commanders for his couches."\par
\par
     "Picky, isn't he?" Taylitha asked, trying for humor and feeling nothing but numb exhaustion. "Why'd you wake me, 'Star?"\par
\par
     "I think I know how you can fend off the approaching ships." The second commander sat beside her more comfortably. She was the only other person in the room; apparently she'd sent the rest of the women out. "It's a legal loophole, but it should work."\par
\par
     "Anything, as long as it keeps us here," Taylitha said.\par
\par
     "The regulation for authority hand-offs during missions-in-progress require the captain or senior commander in orbit to do the actual transfer. You're neither of those people, so you can't do the hand-off."\par
\par
     "I thought in the absence of the captain, authority automatically falls to the next-most-senior commander?" Taylitha said.\par
\par
     "It does . . . if the captain is missing in action."\par
\par
     "Alysha is missing in action," Taylitha said dryly.\par
\par
     "Or maybe she's just in conference with the natives," Alastar said. "Whatever the case, short of declaring this a medical quarantine situation--which any ship captain is going to disbelieve once he comes close enough to notice us continuing to interact with the people on the surface--this is your best bet."\par
\par
     "I'm tired of sort-of lying," Taylitha said. "I've been sort of lying to all the admirals who've come calling. I've been sort of lying to the crew about the crew disappearances. I've been sort of lying to myself about all this coming out okay. Isn't there some way I can tell the truth and have it be enough?"\par
\par
     "And if we found the captain and she told you this was the case?"\par
\par
