 \par
\par
     Working by firelight, the women smoothed the dirt with their hands until the mound they'd constructed seemed to flow into the surrounding land. Even so, Taylitha reflected that she could tell it was a grave. There was some quality to a grave that set it apart even before its tombstone had been set in place.\par
\par
     The woman who placed the marker had carved it herself, using the uncovered reliefs as a guide to the decoration. The metal was stronger than stone, but it had been brushed matte to blend better with the other finds at the nearby sites. When that woman withdrew to join the others standing in a semi-circle around the grave, Taylitha cleared her throat, eyes focused on the small bonfire a few feet away.\par
\par
     "I'm not sure what to say," she said. "Except that even with the best of intentions, sometimes there's nothing we can do. We all have to pass on through the gate to the next life, and all we can ask on that journey is each other's companionship. We came too late to extend the life of this individual . . . but we can hope we made the journey easier."\par
\par
     "Amen," murmured the group.\par
\par
     "That's it, I guess," Taylitha said.\par
\par
     They broke up, some to sit beside the grave, others to wander, ears and tails flagging. Laelkii walked around the grave to stand beside Tayl.\par
\par
     "Sometimes things don't work out the way we planned," Laelkii said.\par
\par
     "Everything that matters should, though," Taylitha said.\par
\par
     "And if they did, we'd only experience the things we wanted to experience," Laelkii said. "We'd get pretty set in our ways then, wouldn't we?"\par
\par
     Taylitha eyed her with a grimace. "And what exactly have I learned here, Snowhide?"\par
\par
     "I'm not sure," Laelkii said. "That you can love someone who can't speak? That you can bury someone and move on? That you can find help in unexpected places? That you can trust people to be what you need?"\par
\par
     "That my best efforts sometimes come to nothing," Taylitha said. "That my resources aren't enough to save the day. That I can fumble my way through a mess of things and not be sure if I did good or bad. That I don't want to be captain yet, or maybe ever."\par
\par
     "Those things too," Laelkii said.\par
\par
     "I don't like these lessons," Taylitha said, folding her arms over her breast.\par
\par
     "Who does?" the older woman said. She patted Taylitha's back and walked off, footsteps crunching in the sandy soil, until she was lost beyond the fire's short circle.\par
\par
     Exasperated, Taylitha called after her, "That wasn't a useful answer!"\par
\par
     Laelkii did not reply. With a sigh, Taylitha sat down across from the grave marker and stared at the soil. She did not want to think too hard about the previous few days; what few images her fuzzy memory relinquished embarrassed her. She'd lost her composure and her equilibrium, plus the first new alien they'd encountered on the planet. Looking back on it, she couldn't imagine doing anything differently . . . but surely something could have been changed. Something would have resulted in a healthy alien to greet Alysha on her return from negotiations in the sea.\par
\par
     They could have looked harder for other aliens on the land. They could have asked the Sunshield's help to cover the available ground. They could have gotten together a group more quickly to help buoy up the alien's fragile mental state. Something would have worked. Something had to have.\par
\par
     What had she done wrong?\par
\par
     She didn't want to cry again, but the first few tears snuck past her defenses anyway. She was just beginning to sniffle when her telegem went off.\par
\par
     "Yes?"\par
\par
     "Sir, it's Admiral Beson from the Exploration Office."\par
\par
     Just what she needed. "Put him through."\par
\par
     "First Commander Basil? What exactly is going on?"\par
\par
     "Sir?" Taylitha asked. Perhaps she'd get lucky and he'd be upset over something she could explain away.\par
\par
     "We've sent the Sunshield but you haven't left yet, and your personnel inform me I still can't speak with the captain . . . which doesn't even address the paper-thinness of your last report to us. I repeat: what's going on?"\par
\par
     Taylitha pressed her thumbs into the inside corners of her eyes. "We'll be on our way shortly, sir, we're just waiting for the captain to conclude negotiations with the aliens."\par
\par
     "The Chatcaava-like beings?"\par
\par
     "No . . . those are dead, sir. The aliens on this planet live in the ocean. They're shaped something like cephalopods."\par
\par
     "There's another set of aliens on the planet?" Beson asked, incredulous.\par
\par
     "There were," Taylitha said. "They're all gone now, sir."\par
\par
     "I thought you said you were talking to them?"\par
\par
     "We found one," Taylitha said. "But he was near death and we didn't succeed in keeping him alive."\par
\par
     Silence. Then, "This is a different story you're telling me now, First Commander."\par
\par
     "And the captain will explain it all in her report for your office, sir," Taylitha said. "I can only relay the bits of it I know, and I haven't known all of it for very long. In fact, I'm completely sure I still don't know the extent of it. But I'm doing my best to hold it together."\par
\par
     Another pause. Did he hear the quiver in her voice, the one she'd tried so hard to suppress? "All right then," the man said. "We'll look forward to a complete explanation of what's gone on."\par
\par
     "Me too," Taylitha said, but the admiral had already chirped out. With a sigh, she rested her head on her arms and blinked, over and over, clearing the water from her eyes.\par
