\par
     "Forrest to Stardancer."\par
\par
     "Alastar here. It's good to hear your voice, sir."\par
\par
     "'Star? Where's Taylitha?"\par
\par
     A hesitation so clear Alysha could see her second commander in her mind's eye, trapped between tact and speech. Then, "She's sleeping. She's had a rough time of it."\par
\par
     "I can imagine," Alysha said. "I didn't intend to leave her in charge with so little notice." She let her lopsided grin touch the words. "Did she survive the experience?"\par
\par
     "She did well," 'Star said firmly. "But it has been difficult."\par
\par
     "I'll talk with her about it, then," Alysha said, her eyes resting on the placid waters, trying to worry them free from the dark horizon. They looked calmer than the conversations that filled them, communicated to her through her submerged feet. "Before I ask for a pr\'e9cis of events . . . you sound well. I trust you're feeling better?"\par
\par
     "Much, thank you. I slept off the worst of the chemical effects, and the rest Laelkii took care of. I imagine you know what happened to me?"\par
\par
     "Their attempt at communication? Yes, I know about it," Alysha said. "I'm glad to hear that Laelkii brushed you off and got you back on your feet. Tell me what's been happening since I've been in the sea."\par
\par
     "We're sharing orbit with the Sunshield, sent to relieve us so we can return for refits," Alastar said. "They're currently working with us at the digs. Personnel ratio is 8 to 1 in favor of their people. Taylitha's discovery of the single native--you know about that?"\par
\par
     "A little," Alysha said.\par
\par
     "He required mental communication with like-minded others to survive, and we could find no others. He died yesterday."\par
\par
     Alysha imagined Taylitha's state of mind and felt a surge of pity. "I'm sorry to hear that."\par
\par
     "We buried him last night; now we're just waiting for you so we can leave. May I ask when you're coming back, sir?"\par
\par
     The tingling excitement in the waters sent shivers up her spine. "Soon," Alysha said. "And I'm bringing the octopi with me. They've decided we're worth their friendship."\par
\par
     "Then they'll join the Alliance?"\par
\par
     "I don't think they'll leave this world," Alysha said, "But yes. I think they'll at very least welcome us as visitors and friends."\par
\par
     "Then there's a happy ending to this after all."\par
\par
     Alysha smiled. "I'll be there today, 'Star. If the Sunshield's here to take care of business, let our people come on holiday. They should be there to greet and celebrate."\par
\par
     "I'll send word, sir."\par
\par
     "I'll see you soon," Alysha said and closed the channel. Behind her Darya and Felsha remained asleep, oblivious to the rising energy level in the water. The sun would wake them soon enough if she didn't; she could just discern there at the corner of the sky the greenish haze that would later give way to full sunrise.\par
\par
     The sun had bulged over the sea's far edge in golden glory when an alien hand stroked her foot. Alysha leaned down and grasped it with both hands, submerging her arms up to the elbow.\par
\par
     Is it done? she asked.\par
\par
     It is done! the guide replied. There will be more octopi within the season. We will live again, and thrive.\par
\par
     Then I am well pleased with what has transpired, Alysha said. Shall we go to meet my people now?\par
\par
     We shall!\par
\par
     Let me wake those who kept vigil with me.\par
\par
     She rose and walked over to the two girls sleeping on the rock. Felsha looked as if she was concentrating on something complex; Darya was curled in an economical shell of a shape, smiling in her sleep. She found that she did not want to touch them, but now was not the time to indulge in petty distastes, particularly those the product of events far gone.\par
\par
     Gently Alysha shook their shoulders, first Felsha, then Darya. "Up, ladies. We're being offered a place in a triumphal procession."\par
\par
     "What? Triumphal procession? There's a parade?" Darya asked, coming away with bleary eyes.\par
\par
     "A parade through cold water to the golden strand between worlds," Alysha said with a laugh. "Of course, if you'd prefer you can stay here until we send someone to pick you up . . . "\par
\par
     "We're up, sir, we're up!" Felsha said, starting out of her tangled position.\par
\par
     Alysha laughed. "Into the water, ladies. We've come a long way to get to this place. Let's make the most of it."\par
\par
     "Yes, sir!"\par
\par
     They gathered their things and stowed them in packs before splashing into the water and grabbing the arm of a guide. Alysha followed at a more leisurely pace, and at the edge of the water stretched, reaching with her full length to the vault of the sky, now streaked with gold, ivory and tender peach. She did not look behind her before wading into the sea.\par
\par
     You are gladsome, the guide said.\par
\par
     If we cannot find happiness in new friendships, what will serve to bring us happiness? Alysha said.\par
\par
     The guide sent a wave of fire-in-the-hearth amusement and began swimming back toward the shore. The wind ruffled her hair as the currents pulled the rest of her body back, and the spray in her face tasted cool and salty. Each new breath seemed to flood her body with energy and a sense of life, of living now.\par
\par
     "Oh, Captain!" Felsha exclaimed. "Look behind us!"\par
\par
     Alysha twisted back and found a row of heads trailing in their wake, several hundred individuals churning the waters. She would not be surprised if every single octopi in the waters had decided to come.\par
\par
     Almost all, her guide said. We left a few to watch over the eggs.\par
\par
     Alysha grinned.\par
\par
     You laugh at the notion that now we do not leave something to the cycle of nature, the guide said.\par
\par
     Perhaps a little, Alysha said. But it is a good-natured laugh, I promise, as I wish only safety for your children.\par
\par
     So do we, the guide said. And that too is in the nature of things. Do you also look after your children until they are old enough to make their own choices?\par
\par
     Of course, Alysha said.\par
\par
     Then we are not so different,.\par
\par
     Have I not said so all along? Alysha said.\par
