\par
     "Are you sure she's coming?" Taylitha asked. "I would hate to have a party without the guests."\par
\par
     "If she doesn't come, we'll eat the food and enjoy it by the sea," 'Star said. "But she is coming. I talked to her just before dawn, local-time. She ordered the party."\par
\par
     And a party required food and laughter and the arrangement of shift schedules so everyone would have a chance to participate without leaving vital functions unstaffed upstairs. Taylitha hadn't even known about the party until an hour ago, but still she'd welcomed the chance to dive into work she understood, that she knew how to do and do well. Logistics she could handle. Everything else she'd give to Alysha when the captain arrived.\par
\par
     The captain had certainly picked a perfect day for a celebration, warm with a cool breeze, with the sky nearly cloudless blue and the water hissing and rolling up the shore. The milling groups from the 'Dancer were mingling with a smaller group from the Sunshield that had also gotten leave. Though she wouldn't admit to having looked, Taylitha had not seen Everen among them.\par
\par
     The crew seemed relaxed; the tragedy of the death of the native had muted everyone's enthusiasm, but word that the captain had contacted a still-viable species in the ocean had restored much of company morale. Combined with the opportunity to relax off-duty with the men of the Sunshield, the news had given the crew back their verve and interest in the digs and the planet's mysteries. It was too bad that they'd have to leave as soon as they completed the hand-off to the Sunshield's teams; everyone wanted to know what new answers would be unearthed with the city.\par
\par
     A few of the woman began to point at the sea. As Taylitha turned to follow their interest, 'Star said, "Here they come."\par
\par
     Bobbing beneath the waves with only their heads and luminous eyes in view came the Stardancer's first, living alien species. Taylitha's breath caught in her throat at the sight of their iridescent skin and present eyes. How often had she secretly dreamed of first contact? This was how those dreams had always ended . . . a successful friendship initiated, a new species suddenly a part of the colorful tapestry of the Alliance. This was the way things were supposed to go . . . and she was here to see it.\par
\par
     Alysha walked out of the waters, dripping, her hair streaming. She should have looked ridiculous, like a water-logged cat . . . instead, she looked composed, fierce, alive and full of laughter. Taylitha waited for her to burst into that laughter, but she didn't. Instead, she turned as one of the octopi left the waters with her and arrayed itself on the shore by her side. The bright morning's sunlight shone off the iridescent apron connecting its tentacles as if it were made of gold.\par
\par
     "Ladies," Alysha said simply, "Our newest friends."\par
\par
     A wild cheer went up from the onlookers. From behind Alysha, two more figures trudged out of the waters. Darya said, "Well, what are you people waiting for? Come for a swim and say hello!"\par
