 \par
\par
     "Sugar?" Alysha said from the sideboard.\par
\par
     "No, thanks. I'll take it black today."\par
\par
     Alysha nodded and brought two mugs with her back to the coffee table in the center of her quarters' receiving room. Taylitha was curled up on the sofa, her head propped up by her hands.\par
\par
     "I've reviewed your report," Alysha said, offering her the mug. "You did an excellent job with a very difficult situation."\par
\par
     "I don't feel like I did an excellent job," Taylitha said. She took the mug and cradled it in her hands, blocking the Alliance's moon-and-stars-and-pennant. "Someone died. On my watch."\par
\par
     "Laelkii calls that an inevitability," Alysha said, sitting across from the other woman.\par
\par
     "She's just trying to be nice," Taylitha said.\par
\par
     Alysha chuckled softly. "Snowhide is a gentle and compassionate woman, arii, and that tends to make people forget that she's also a doctor. If she says your alien wasn't going to make it, she's not trying to coddle you, she's giving her medical opinion."\par
\par
     Taylitha said nothing, staring into her mug.\par
\par
     "You did what you could with what you had," Alysha said, gentling her voice, "and you kept things together. That's nothing to turn your face from."\par
\par
     "I didn't do it with much grace," Taylitha said. "Running around the corridors, never getting enough sleep, ducking calls from admirals, crying into the shoulders of people I barely know--"\par
\par
     "Sending reports, keeping people on track, caring for a dying individual, overseeing morale and relying on others so as not to squander your own strength," Alysha said. "Just because you didn't do it perfectly doesn't mean you didn't do it well. It was your first time handling a situation of this complexity, and while I have suggestions on how to make it easier I have no complaints. What needed to be done got done. What didn't, didn't."\par
\par
     Taylitha said nothing, but her ears flushed.\par
\par
     "And I wouldn't worry about Captain Everen," Alysha said. "He likes your style."\par
\par
     "My style!" Taylitha blurted, then started laughing. "I think the only thing he saw me do was cry."\par
\par
     "Actually, he saw you handle a difficult situation with strength and compassion," Alysha said, "and as for the crying . . . it's no flaw to release your own sorrow once you've taken care of everything that needed it."\par
\par
     "And you do so much crying," Taylitha said, ears flattening.\par
\par
     Alysha sipped from her mug and said, "What is it that's really bothering you, arii?"\par
\par
     Taylitha looked away. "I just feel like . . . I let him get too close. I should have been more distant. Then I could have handled him dying better." She sniffled. "I'm still not handling him dying very well. I barely knew him!"\par
\par
     "You knew him well enough," Alysha said. She sighed and put her mug on the table. "Can I tell you a secret, Taylitha?"\par
\par
     Taylitha looked up.\par
\par
     "I had to stand back and let the oldest member of an alien species commit suicide," Alysha said. "And I don't feel all that great about it."\par
\par
     "The Eldest?" Taylitha said. "I thought that in laying her eggs she would have died anyway."\par
\par
     "Yes," Alysha said. "But to give herself up to the leviathan -- I had to let her make that choice."\par
\par
     "It's not like you could have done any differently," Taylitha said. "From the report, you barely made a dent in that thing. Even if you'd wanted to, you couldn't have protected her."\par
\par
     "No," Alysha said, feeling a sudden wave of cold.\par
\par
     "Oh, Bast and An," Taylitha said, ears flattening. "I'm so sorry, arii. I know that's one of those things with you. But . . . I really don't see how you could have done anything differently. And you're handling it a lot better than I'm handling my problem."\par
\par
     "Am I?" Alysha said and sighed. "I ended up in the water, where I had to stand aside from someone and let them choose the way they spent their life. You ended up on land, where you had to give yourself entirely to someone without regard of the cost to yourself. I would have rathered it been the other way around."\par
